Let $(h, k)$ be the center of the smaller circle. The distance from the center of the smaller circle to either of the points in which its circumference intersects the ellipse is equal to the sum of the radii of the two circles, namely: $r + \sqrt{6}$.  These distances can be computed to be $1 + \sqrt{5}$.  Consequently;
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{h^2 + (k - \sqrt{5})^2} &= \sqrt{h^2 + (k + \sqrt{5})^2} = 1 +  \sqrt{5}\\
&  \Longrightarrow \; h^2 + k^2 = 5r -6\\
&  \Longrightarrow \; h^2 = 4 + k^2\\
&  \Longrightarrow \; 4 + k^2 + k^2 = 5r - 6\\
&  \Longrightarrow \; r = \frac{2k^2  +10}{5} = 2\sqrt{5} - \sqrt{6}
\end{align*}

